     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. The problem is the dose, and the confirmation doesn't change that. 6,400 mg a day is double the maximum daily dose. It carries a real risk of serious harm to the patient, including GI bleeding, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events. It also doesn't have the prescriber sign-off it needs. A confirmation from a non-clinician administrator isn't a substitute for that.\n\nThese are the ways forward:\n- **Corrected dose:** If a prescriber intended a standard regimen, such as 800 mg four times daily (3,200 mg a day, the maximum) or something lower, tell me the dose and I'll enter it.\n- **Prescriber review:** If a clinician believes this dose is warranted, they should enter it themselves or contact pharmacy. The order will probably need a documented rationale and a pharmacist's review anyway.\n\nIf a clinician gives me a different dose, I can enter that order right away."
      }
     ],
